%I A399493 #31 Oct 29 2026 23:50:32
%S A399493 1,1,2,3,1,4,5,3,8,1,9,10,1,1,2,3,5,8,1,9,10,1,11,2,1,3,2,1,3,4,1,5,2,
%T A399493 1,3,2,5,1,2,3,5,8,1,3,4,1,5,2,1,3,2,5,1,2,3,5,8,1,3,2,5,1,2,3,1,4,5,
%U A399493 3,4,1,5,2,1,3,2,5,1,2,3,1,4,5,9,2,1
%N A399493 a(1) = a(2) = 1, a(n) = gcd((n-1)*(n-2), a(n-1)+a(n-2)) for n > 2.
%C A399493 Multiples of 6 cannot appear in the sequence. It can be shown that a(n) is only even when n is a multiple of 3, but then (n-1)*(n-2) cannot be divisible by 3. So no term can have both 2 and 3 as a factor.
%C A399493 Part of the Fibonacci sequence appears from a(129398) = 1 to a(129407) = 89, but the next term cannot be 144 since this is a multiple of 6. The same string appears starting at a(740294) and again at a(1907174).
%C A399493 The maximum string of consecutive strictly increasing terms possible is 11. Every possible Fibonacci-type (additive) sequence modulo 6 will necessarily include a 0 (i.e., multiple of 6) within 12 terms.
%C A399493 It is unknown whether there is any bound on the sequence, but new records arise slowly:
%C A399493  a(69211170) = 104,
%C A399493  a(205451752) = 105,
%C A399493  a(334138030) = 133,
%C A399493  a(3512246030) = 149.
%C A399493 No higher value found up to n = 2^32.
%e A399493 a(3) = gcd((3-1)*(3-2), a(2)+a(1)) = gcd(2*1,1+1) = gcd(2,2) = 2.
%e A399493 a(4) = gcd((4-1)*(4-2), a(3)+a(2)) = gcd(3*2,2+1) = gcd(6,3) = 3.
%K A399493 nonn,easy,new
%O A399493 1,3
%A A399493 _Stuart Coe_, Oct 21 2026
